; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Undo and Redo
; CATEGORY: Lists & Data Structures
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     stack, undo, redo, keyboard, run
; SUMMARY:  Drive the turtle with keys and use two stacks to undo and redo your moves, just like a text editor does.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Every move you make is pushed onto a HISTORY stack as a small
;   Logo instruction list such as [fd 30]. Pressing U pops the last
;   move and pushes it onto a REDO stack, then the picture is redrawn
;   from the history. Pressing R pops from REDO and does the move
;   again. Making a fresh move empties REDO, which is exactly what
;   your word processor does too.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Click the turtle world first so it hears the keyboard.
;   F = forward, L = turn left, T = turn right,
;   U = undo, R = redo, C = change color, Q = quit.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * two stacks working together
;   * storing instructions as data, then running them with RUN
;   * redrawing a picture by replaying a list
;   * READCHAR for single key presses
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add a B key that moves backward.
;   * Print the history after each key to watch the stack.
;   * Make U undo two moves at once.
; ============================================================

make "history []
make "redo []
make "colors [blue red forest purple orange]

; Redraw from scratch. The history keeps the NEWEST move first, so we
; replay it reversed to repeat the moves in the order they happened.
to replay
  clearscreen
  setpensize 3 setpencolor "blue
  foreach reverse :history [run ?]
  showturtle
end

; A brand new move: do it, remember it, and forget any redo moves,
; because they belonged to a different future.
to domove :instr
  run :instr
  push "history :instr
  make "redo []
end

to undo
  if emptyp :history [print [Nothing to undo.] stop]
  push "redo pop "history
  replay
end

to redo
  if emptyp :redo [print [Nothing to redo.] stop]
  localmake "instr pop "redo
  run :instr
  push "history :instr
end

; Turn the key into a move. CASE compares without caring about case,
; so f and F both work.
to handle :key
  case :key [
    [[f] domove [fd 30]]
    [[l] domove [lt 45]]
    [[t] domove [rt 45]]
    [[c] domove (list "setpencolor quoted pick :colors)]
    [[u] undo]
    [[r] redo]
  ]
end

to play
  print [Click the turtle world first, then press keys.]
  print [F forward, L left, T right, C color, U undo, R redo, Q quit]
  forever [
    localmake "key readchar
    if emptyp :key [print [No more keys.] stop]
    if equalp lowercase :key "q [
      print (sentence [You made] count :history [moves.])
      stop
    ]
    handle lowercase :key
  ]
end

; A little starting drawing so there is something to undo right away.
replay
foreach [[fd 60] [rt 90] [fd 60] [rt 90] [fd 60]] [domove ?]
play
